TOOL SPEC + ESSAY MATERIAL — 'oncompute' and the frictionless-wrong-path problem.

FOLLOW-UP TOOL (agreed 2026-08-16, skill written, tool deferred):
  oncompute <path> -- <cmd...>
Resolve the owning host from the mount table (findmnt -T <path> gives host AND
remote path directly, so no hardcoded table needed), translate the pip-side path
to the host-local path, then ssh + nohup + python -u against LOCAL storage.
Decision: compute host is ALWAYS the data owner (not pog/nit) — confirmed by
Peter. Log to ~/tmp/ never /tmp. Design questions left: progress streaming back,
how to name/poll the job, whether it wraps or just prints the command.

ESSAY POINT — why conventions break, and what that says about tooling:
Peter: 'the convention is broken all the time'. Investigating WHY produced the
best material of the session. The NFS mounts are x-systemd.automount, so
/mnt/muppet/bigstore/... just works on pip — no mount step, no error, no
ceremony, tab-completion walks straight into it. The WRONG PATH IS FRICTIONLESS
AND INVISIBLE. So the 15GB-over-wifi mistake has no moment of decision at which
to apply discipline. Measured cost: 4.9 MB/s I/O-starved on pip vs ~34 MB/s at
90% CPU on muppet — 7x, plus it saturates the link Peter works over and cooks
the laptop.

The general claim: A CONVENTION THAT IS BROKEN CONSTANTLY IS USUALLY COMPETING
WITH AN EASIER WRONG PATH. The fix is not more discipline, and not a louder
rule in always-loaded context — it is either (a) a skill that fires on a
MECHANICALLY VISIBLE TELL rather than on intent, or (b) removing the friction
asymmetry so the right thing is the easy thing. Discipline is the most expensive
and least reliable of the three, and it is exactly what a one-line rule in
GLOBAL.md asks for.

TRIGGER DESIGN — the transferable bit. My first instinct was to trigger on
DURATION ('jobs over ~1min'), which is unworkable: duration is discovered at
second 90, when the laptop is already hot and the skill's moment has passed.
The working trigger is DATA LOCALITY — a path under /mnt/<host>/ — because it is
visible in the command BEFORE it runs and is mechanically checkable. General
rule: TRIGGER ON WHAT IS VISIBLE AT WRITE-TIME, NOT ON WHAT IS ONLY KNOWABLE AT
RUN-TIME. Same reasoning applies to any 'expensive operation' guard.

DEPRECATION AS A TRAP: Peter said 'use cpuworker to test this algorithm', but
cpuworker is deprecated (2026-05-13) and does the OPPOSITE — replicated scp
pushing files outward so every worker holds every file. Hanging the skill on
that name would have taught both of us to reach for the wrong deprecated tool.
The vocabulary Peter uses for an intention can outlive the tool that once served
it. Worth a line in the essay: naming a skill after a tool binds it to that
tool's lifecycle; naming it after the PRINCIPLE (compute-follows-the-data)
survives the tool being replaced. The skill therefore triggers on the phrase
'use cpuworker' but redirects.

